WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:09.510
PROFESSOR: Hello.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.650
In this lecture, we talk about
parity and parity violation.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.530
So we talk about a
discrete symmetry.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.335
A little bit of history
in particle physics--

00:00:21.335 --> 00:00:25.850
Lee and Yang in
the 1950s wondered

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.870
if there's any experiment
testing parity invariance.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.210
There are many tests
which had to do

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.850
with a strong interaction
and the electromagnetic

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:35.900
interaction.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.900
But as it turned out,
parity invariance

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.790
had never been tested
in the weak interaction.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.660
And so Lee and Yang
were motivated in this

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.985
because it was a
puzzle at the time,

00:00:46.985 --> 00:00:53.045
so-called tau-theta puzzle,
which turned out to be kaon

00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:56.270
decays into various articles.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:01.100
And then that puzzle,
they solved the case

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.620
with the same lifetime,
but different spin states.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.080
And so they couldn't
quite understand this,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.150
and were trying to
understand whether or not

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.060
one can have an
experiment to test this.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.370
The experiments
they proposed is one

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:21.260
where you look at the nuclei
and observe beta decay.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.020
So there's an electron
coming out of the beta decay.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.020
If you are able to align
the spin of the nuclei

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.000
and put it in front
of a mirror, you

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.010
see that the physics
going to the mirror state

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.570
is not [INAUDIBLE] parity.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.340
You see that in the mirror, the
spin is the opposite direction,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.620
but the momentum
of the electrons,

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.900
the electrons still
come out on the bottom.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.460
So there's clearly some
change in the physics going on

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.830
in the mirrored state.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.240
So Madame Wu actually
took up this idea,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.270
and immediately tested
this in the same year.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.230
She was a Chinese-American
physicist, born in China,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:08.870
and then studied in Berkeley
together with famous people

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.580
there.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.920
Lawrence is one of them.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.700
After the war or in the war,
she joined the Manhattan Project

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.610
and made very, very
important contribution

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:24.560
to the Manhattan Project
based on her thesis work.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.910
As a fun fact, she was married
to another Chinese-American,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.480
the grandson of
the first President

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.330
of the Republic of China.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:39.710
As I said, in 1956, she
conducted the Wu experiment,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.960
and in the following year
received the Nobel Prize

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.240
in physics for the finding.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.910
So really briefly, we're going
to have another discussion

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.850
of the Wu experiment later on.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.720
What she did here is she
studied cobalt 60 decay

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.240
to nickel, beta decays.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.730
And what she was able
to do experimentally

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.880
with the magnetic field,
align the cobalt 60,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.820
and then just count the number
of electrons coming out.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.670
And it turns out that the
number of electrons coming out

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.920
of the bottom and the
top are not the same.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.530
She did this by reverting
a magnetic field.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.570
And the experimental
data is shown here.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:22.130
You see as a function
of time, you just

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.050
look at counting, which
is a function of time

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:27.000
after injecting the
probe, and you revert.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.440
You do the measurement by
reverting the magnetic field.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.950
And you see very clearly
here that there's asymmetry

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.120
in the beta decays.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.610
And that asymmetry
immediately tells you

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:42.170
that there has to be parity
violation in beta decays

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.810
or in weak interaction.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:51.550
So she found that this specific
picture here violates parity.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.730
And this is a dramatic signature
of the weak interaction.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.720
And we will later see how we
can actually understand it.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.345
Note that parity is conserved
in electromagnetic interaction

00:04:02.345 --> 00:04:05.270
and strong interaction.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.640
One more word on
parity inversion--

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.750
you can define a
parity operator.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.200
And if you apply this parity
operator, for example,

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.260
on the vector, these
x, y, and z components

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.929
you simply turn the direction
over here with a minus sign.

